From bf96a18de270751b908a1adcdeed0c478f7f77ee Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: yekai pan <pyk@seas.upenn.edu>
Date: Tue, 6 Oct 2026 04:10:28 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 02/16] [ffi] mask tag bits before accessing foreign reference
 counts

A `tagged` foreign handle that is not an immediate points to a box whose
`u32` count is at offset 0. On 64-bit targets the foreign side may also
keep data in the top 16 bits of such a handle, as runtimes with tagged
pointers do. `rc.inc` now clears them (`llvm.ptrmask`, which keeps the
provenance) before it loads and stores the count, on every path: plain,
with a delta, and atomic. `FFIObjectType::TAGGED_ADDRESS_BITS` (48) names
the width of the address. The cleanup hook still gets the handle
unchanged, so the foreign side sees its own encoding.

Without the mask the increment reads and writes an address with those
bits set, which faults on x86-64 and on aarch64 (only the top byte is
ignored there). A handle with clear top bits is unchanged, at the cost of
one AND on the increment of a box; immediates never reach it. Untagged
objects and targets narrower than 64 bits lower as before. No other code
reads through a tagged handle.

Tests: conversion/tagged_ffi_object.mlir (the mask before the load and the
store, also with a delta and before the atomicrmw of an atomic count; none
for an untagged object; the hook gets the unmasked handle).
